WEBVTT

00:03.880 --> 00:06.800
All right, moving on to the next use case for the coalesce and Isnull.

00:06.960 --> 00:12.520
We can use them in order to handle the nulls before doing any mathematical operations.

00:12.520 --> 00:15.440
So let's understand what this means using the plus operator.

00:15.440 --> 00:20.680
So if you do plus operator between two numbers like one plus five, you are summarizing the values and

00:20.680 --> 00:21.720
you will get six.

00:21.720 --> 00:26.480
And if you do the plus operator between string values like a plus b.

00:26.480 --> 00:31.320
So now what we are doing, we are doing data concatenations and the output is going to be a B.

00:31.360 --> 00:36.840
So now if you go and replace the one with value like zero, so zero plus five we will get five.

00:36.880 --> 00:38.440
Nothing fancy about that.

00:38.440 --> 00:44.200
And for the strings if you go and replace a value with an empty string, so there is zero characters

00:44.200 --> 00:50.640
between the two quotes plus the b, so in the output you will get only B, so it's fine and nothing

00:50.640 --> 00:51.480
is critical.

00:51.480 --> 00:52.800
But now we come to the problem.

00:52.800 --> 00:58.480
If you use a null, if you replace the one with a null in the output, you will get a null because you

00:58.520 --> 01:01.800
are saying okay, five plus something that I don't know.

01:01.800 --> 01:06.020
So SQL scale says, okay, you are summarizing now a value with a null value.

01:06.060 --> 01:06.940
It is unknown.

01:06.940 --> 01:09.540
So I don't as well know what's going to be the answer.

01:09.540 --> 01:11.500
That's why it's going to say it's going to be null.

01:11.580 --> 01:13.540
SQL just don't know what is the answer.

01:13.540 --> 01:16.260
And the same thing can happen with anything else like the string.

01:16.260 --> 01:22.740
So if you are saying null plus b and he is going to say the same thing, the null is unknown and the

01:22.740 --> 01:24.580
answer is going to be as well unknown.

01:24.580 --> 01:28.860
So my friends, this is very critical in the analysis and working with data.

01:28.860 --> 01:33.180
So this means we have to handle the nulls before doing any mathematical operations.

01:33.180 --> 01:35.380
And this is not only for the plus operator.

01:35.620 --> 01:38.580
It's as well for the other operators like minus and so on.

01:38.580 --> 01:38.860
All right.

01:38.860 --> 01:40.140
So now let's have the following task.

